# How Godot's SSR Reflects a Room {#video subtitle="Screen-space reflections in Godot 4.7, followed through the source and ported pass by pass" engineVersion=4.7.2-stable commit=ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079 date=2026-11-03 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where SSR runs {#c1 number=1 target=150 summary="What SSR adds, the problem it solves, the passes of Godot's frame it is made of, and what switches it on." sources="gating, ssr-normal-roughness, process-ssr, copy-last-frame, env-defaults, half-size-setting"}

### SSR puts the room into the floor {#c1-adds kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {' {ssrEnabled 0.0} {floorRoughness 0.04} {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.45 -0.8}} {cameraYaw 14.0} {cameraPitch 6.0} {cameraDistance 4.6}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 14.0 6.0 4.6} {' 22.0 7.0 4.3} {' 30.0 8.0 4.6} {' 20.0 6.0 4.4}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraPitch} {' {' 7.0}} sentence}}}}}This is the lab's study room, its floor a sheet of polished metal, and with nothing to reflect it is black.

${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 0.0 1.0}} "Now Godot's screen-space reflections are on"}: ${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 26.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.04 0.02}} "the sphere, the blue box, and the red and green walls appear in the floor"}.

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.02 0.35}} "As the floor grows rougher, the reflections blur"}, ${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.35 0.75}} "and at a roughness of seven tenths they stop altogether"}.

### The sky cannot see the teapot {#c1-problem kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoCompose panelHidden {append baseRoom {append lowCamera {' {skyReflection 1.0} {ssrComposeStep 1.0} {floorRoughness 0.04}}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 12.0 8.0} {' 16.0 9.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {skyReflectionRange 0.0 1.0}} "Without it, the floor of this room can only reflect the sky, which knows nothing of the room's objects"}.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraYaw} {' {' 22.0}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {ssrComposeStepRange 1.0 3.0}} "The question is how the floor can reflect what is already on screen, without tracing the scene again"}.

${cue "key" {' {ssrComposeStep 3.0} {skyReflection 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.04 0.02}} "By the end you will be able to explain the depth pyramid, the ray marched through it"}, ${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.02 0.3}} "where its colour comes from, and how it is blurred and blended"}.

### The passes, in Godot's frame {#c1-map kind=2d graph=frame focus="SSR"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-ssr"}}} Before the opaque pass, by default five passes build the reflections: a downsample, a depth pyramid, the trace, a blur, and a resolve.

### Ordered by what they read and write {#c1-graph kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelDag baseRoom}}
${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace"}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 24.0 12.0} {' 20.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrPrepassFS"}}}}A frame is a list of passes, ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample"}}}}and one may start only when what it reads is written.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz"}}}}The pyramid pass writes the depth pyramid, ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace"}}}}and the trace reads it, ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace"}}}}so the trace runs after it.

${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample" "ssrHiz"}}}}Following those arrows ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrTrace" "ssrFilter"}}}}from pass to pass ${cue "key" {' {highlight {' "ssrDownsample" "ssrHiz" "ssrTrace" "ssrFilter" "ssrResolve"}}}}gives the whole order.

### Last frame closes a loop {#c1-history kind=2d graph=frame focus="HISTORY, SSR"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "copy-last-frame"}}} One arrow runs backwards: after the transparent pass the frame is copied, and the next frame's trace reads that copy, a resource kept from frame to frame.

### What switches it on {#c1-settings kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelValues {append baseRoom {' {floorRoughness 0.04}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {' 18.0 10.0} {' 22.0 14.0} {' 16.0 11.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "env-defaults"}}}${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 1.0 0.0}} "SSR is off by default"}; ${over {' {ssrEnabledRange 0.0 1.0}} {overText {' {ssrMaxStepsRange 32.0 64.0}} "the Environment turns it on, with sixty-four steps, a fade in of fifteen hundredths, a fade out of two, and a depth tolerance of half a unit"}}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "half-size-setting"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.2}} sentence}}}}}A project setting runs it at half resolution, and that is the default.

${cue "key" {' {callout "gating"}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}${overText {' {ssrEnabledRange 1.0 0.0}} "It runs in the Forward+ renderer only, and never for a reflection probe or a transparent background"}.

### Next: what the trace reads {#c1-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom lowCamera}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPath {' {' 18.0 10.0 5.6} {' 30.0 14.0 5.0} {' 24.0 11.0 5.3}}}}}}}

${overText {' {floorRoughnessRange 0.15 0.05}} "So SSR is a chain of passes before the opaque pass, fed by last frame's image"}.

${cue "key" {' {ssrEnabled 1.0} {floorRoughness 0.1} {blend 1.5}}}${cue "key" {' {motion {' {path {' cameraDistance} {' {' 5.6}} sentence}}}}}Each pass starts from two images of what is on screen, which the next chapter takes apart.

